WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.866
Dorothy and the Scarecrow were amazed.

2
00:00:02.867 --> 00:00:06.151
Standing in the forest was a man made of tin.

3
00:00:06.152 --> 00:00:09.578
"Did you groan?" asked Dorothy.

4
00:00:09.579 --> 00:00:12.358
"Yes," the Tin Man answered sadly.

5
00:00:12.359 --> 00:00:17.737
"I have been groaning for over a year. You are the first to hear me."

6
00:00:17.738 --> 00:00:24.402
"How can we help you?" asked Dorothy softly, because his voice sounded so sad.

7
00:00:24.403 --> 00:00:28.179
"Oil . . . oil my joints," said the Tin Man.

8
00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:31.360
"They are rusted. I cannot move them."

9
00:00:31.361 --> 00:00:37.449
"Sir, I have no oil. I only have bread in my basket," said Dorothy.

10
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:41.119
"There's an oilcan in my cottage," said the Tin Man.

11
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.026
"Get it, and you can make me better."

12
00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:48.791
Dorothy ran to the cottage and brought the oilcan back.

13
00:00:48.792 --> 00:00:51.973
"Where do you need oil?" asked Dorothy.

14
00:00:51.974 --> 00:00:59.220
"Do my neck first," said the Tin Man. "Ah . . . now do my arms."

15
00:00:59.221 --> 00:01:06.335
As soon as his arms were free, the Tin Man lowered his ax and leaned it against a tree.

16
00:01:06.336 --> 00:01:12.518
"I've been holding that ax for more than a year. Do my legs," he continued.

17
00:01:12.519 --> 00:01:15.685
Dorothy and the Scarecrow did as they were asked.

18
00:01:15.686 --> 00:01:21.344
The Tin Man gave a cry of joy. "Thank you! Thank you!"

19
00:01:21.345 --> 00:01:23.893
"You're welcome," said Dorothy.

20
00:01:23.894 --> 00:01:29.863
"You're lucky we were on our way to see Oz, a great wizard, or we wouldn't have heard you."

21
00:01:29.864 --> 00:01:33.654
"Why are you going to see Oz?" he asked.

22
00:01:33.655 --> 00:01:41.078
"I want to go back to Kansas, and the Scarecrow wants a brain rather than straw in his head," she replied.

23
00:01:41.079 --> 00:01:46.878
The Tin Man thought for a moment and asked, "Do you think Oz would give me a heart?"

24
00:01:46.879 --> 00:01:54.087
"Maybe," answered Dorothy. "Hearts are probably as easy as brains for the Wizard of Oz."

25
00:01:54.088 --> 00:01:57.604
"May I join you?" asked the Tin Man.

26
00:01:57.605 --> 00:02:01.100
"Yes, come along," said the Scarecrow.

27
00:02:01.101 --> 00:02:03.743
"Yes!" agreed Dorothy.

28
00:02:03.744 --> 00:02:08.803
So the Tin Man shouldered his ax, and they all followed the Yellow Brick Road.

29
00:02:08.804 --> 00:02:13.868
Soon Dorothy was bored because there was nothing much to see on the long journey.

30
00:02:13.869 --> 00:02:17.150
So she asked, "Who made you, Tin Man?

31
00:02:17.151 --> 00:02:22.084
Scarecrow was made by a farmer. Were you made by a woodcutter?"

32
00:02:22.085 --> 00:02:26.246
"No, I was a woodcutter's son," said the Tin Man.

33
00:02:26.247 --> 00:02:30.068
"How did you become a Tin Man?" she asked.

34
00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:35.747
"It's a love story with a curse by the Wicked Witch of the East," he replied.

35
00:02:35.748 --> 00:02:38.900
"Please tell us," said Dorothy.

36
00:02:38.901 --> 00:02:43.004
The Tin Man was quiet for a moment and then began.

37
00:02:43.005 --> 00:02:48.147
"I fell in love with a beautiful girl, and I asked her to marry me."

38
00:02:48.148 --> 00:02:52.249
"What did she say?" asked Dorothy.

39
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:57.744
"She said she would marry me when I had enough money to buy a house," the Tin Man replied.

40
00:02:57.745 --> 00:03:00.293
"Her stepmother was a lazy woman.

41
00:03:00.294 --> 00:03:07.230
She wanted her stepdaughter to stay with her and do all the housework, so she asked the Wicked Witch to prevent the marriage."

42
00:03:07.231 --> 00:03:10.525
"What happened?" asked Dorothy.

43
00:03:10.526 --> 00:03:14.037
"The witch enchanted my ax," said the Tin Man.

44
00:03:14.038 --> 00:03:18.294
"One day when I was working, the ax cut off my leg.

45
00:03:18.295 --> 00:03:21.621
I went to a tinsmith, and he made me a leg.

46
00:03:21.622 --> 00:03:25.033
I went back to work again, and the ax slipped.

47
00:03:25.034 --> 00:03:29.415
I had lost my other leg, but my love made me strong.

48
00:03:29.416 --> 00:03:34.080
The tinsmith made me another leg, and I went back to work.

49
00:03:34.081 --> 00:03:38.288
The next time the ax slipped, I lost both my arms."

50
00:03:38.289 --> 00:03:41.275
"Oh no!" exclaimed Dorothy.

51
00:03:41.276 --> 00:03:46.737
"Thanks to my love and the tinsmith, I was soon back at work," continued the Tin Man.

52
00:03:46.738 --> 00:03:51.988
"This angered the witch, so the next time the ax took my head.

53
00:03:51.989 --> 00:03:57.529
I thought that was the end, but the tinsmith made me a head, and I went back to work.

54
00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:01.607
When the ax struck again, I was cut in two.

55
00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:06.819
The tinsmith made a body out of tin, but the body had no heart.

56
00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:10.913
Without a heart I had lost my love for the beautiful girl.

57
00:04:10.914 --> 00:04:13.732
That is why I want one."

58
00:04:13.733 --> 00:04:18.067
Dorothy was very moved by the story, but she was also worried.

59
00:04:18.068 --> 00:04:24.657
There was very little bread left in her basket.

